#{[17592186372153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472074 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472075 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472076 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=3x\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=x\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f67f3f6-b8ed-585a-bae8-a685dca4c830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3+(-2y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+2xy\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472077 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472078 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472079 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=4{y}^{2}x\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$\n- **III.** $F(x,y)=2yx\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "169dc9ad-6608-5ab8-8ec5-7973ca027bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4{y}^{2}+(-2xy)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2y+(-2y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(0,\\pi )$ for the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd6e61d1-6465-500e-9577-5af9c54bc069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(0,\\pi ) & ={e}^{(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is the $0$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our possible streamlines are $y=k\\pi $ for any integer $k.$ The streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is $y=\\pi .$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bd732367a876d2e6bba6bf96728ebcf8430176d2f480bff30a054cac5389b1f2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1626cb5c-1d71-5bed-88ae-3afa894a0595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372183 #edb/ref 1080 "x=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372183 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372183 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3041c7b2-ad5d-59e4-a35f-cdd3f5a8ded4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372184 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\pi {e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e0bfc27-933c-52a6-a9da-b121d5422481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372185 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b692407-30c1-5d1f-ac8b-4238f37dea05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372186 #edb/ref 1080 "x=\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dbbfc69-0b08-5509-a6ec-62eeb283eb76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372187 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372187 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372187 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d8466d6-4bb0-570a-a74f-a68de142e108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b93df85-0b3c-5ded-8201-a97d95285f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce55e1ed-b422-5201-909f-65fba9887b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389341 #edb/ref 1080 "images/6a/6a1ce9b96f0bca4ec14e7ce60e5cebd72efc4421acfd4a65ba55e9c8a7d1d352.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389341 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc9bdb8d-ad2b-5d91-86ca-825ec11037fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389342 #edb/ref 1080 "images/07/0799c425e6b33f2be8f115d7d0de9e5607bdef9f094a02381fc5197839d80d75.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389342 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389342 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "678a4e30-3832-553e-8ea1-b4ab930f1cd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389343 #edb/ref 1080 "images/95/95fee99b51dc07130717569fcb90ab5e210bf2cc64811f536dc493e9c3e1588e.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389343 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389343 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6db4de8e-dbc7-51ee-8ae9-5b089f44fbf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389344 #edb/ref 1080 "images/3a/3a3ef451c7e25ff33fb1820d5b8c98e10038c4ee058f627d93acf72989b7b794.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389344 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389344 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92bd4bba-9b1d-550f-a3d8-850c5ca8fd4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389345 #edb/ref 1080 "images/fb/fb7119466ac4f884fca55cfb3fc0b487703249050211ff922549a702a047bb39.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389345 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389345 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bc3c2dd-f56c-5123-bcfe-941b923629fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389346 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389346 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389346 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389346 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389346 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc28542a-a43a-5f01-b243-0063900eefa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389346 #edb/ref 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389348 #edb/ref 175921860454